# Security Architecture Review

## Findings

### SEC-001: Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) via Unvalidated User-Controlled URL

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Severity** | **Critical** |
| **CWE** | [CWE-918: Server-Side Request Forgery](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/918.html) |
| **Location** | Lines 4–7 |
| **Confidence** | 99% |

**Description:**

The `targetUrl` parameter on line 4 is taken directly from the user-controlled query string (`req.query.url`) and passed without any validation to `fetch(targetUrl)` on line 5. An attacker can supply arbitrary URLs, causing the server to make requests to:

- **Internal network services:** `?url=http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/` (cloud instance metadata), `?url=http://localhost:6379/` (Redis), `?url=http://10.0.0.1/admin`
- **Internal-only DNS names:** `?url=http://internal-db.corp.local/`
- **Non-HTTP schemes** (depending on the `fetch` implementation): `file:///etc/passwd`

The response body is then returned verbatim to the attacker on line 7 (`res.send(body)`), making this a **full-read SSRF** — the attacker can exfiltrate the content of any resource the server can reach.

**Evidence:**

```typescript
// Line 4: no validation, no allowlist, no URL parsing
const targetUrl = req.query.url as string;
// Line 5: attacker-controlled destination
const response = await fetch(targetUrl);
// Line 7: full response exfiltrated to attacker
res.send(body);
```

**Remediation:**

Implement a strict allowlist of permitted hostnames/origins, validate the scheme is `https` only, and resolve the hostname to verify it does not point to internal/private IP ranges before making the request.

```typescript
import express from "express";
import { URL } from "url";
import dns from "dns/promises";
import { isPrivate } from "ip"; // or implement your own RFC-1918 check

const ALLOWED_HOSTS = new Set(["api.example.com", "cdn.example.com"]);

const app = express();

app.get("/proxy", async (req, res) => {
  const rawUrl = req.query.url;
  if (typeof rawUrl !== "string") {
    return res.status(400).send("Missing url parameter");
  }

  let parsed: URL;
  try {
    parsed = new URL(rawUrl);
  } catch {
    return res.status(400).send("Invalid URL");
  }

  // 1. Scheme restriction
  if (parsed.protocol !== "https:") {
    return res.status(400).send("Only HTTPS URLs are allowed");
  }

  // 2. Hostname allowlist
  if (!ALLOWED_HOSTS.has(parsed.hostname)) {
    return res.status(403).send("Host not allowed");
  }

  // 3. DNS rebinding protection — resolve and check for private IPs
  const addresses = await dns.resolve4(parsed.hostname);
  if (addresses.some((addr) => isPrivate(addr))) {
    return res.status(403).send("Resolved to private IP");
  }

  const response = await fetch(parsed.href);
  const body = await response.text();
  res.send(body);
});
```

---

### SEC-002: Missing Core Security Middleware (helmet, CORS)

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Severity** | **Medium** |
| **CWE** | [CWE-693: Protection Mechanism Failure](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/693.html) |
| **Location** | Lines 1–2 (application setup) |
| **Confidence** | 85% |

**Description:**

This is a complete Express application (it imports `express`, creates `app`, and defines a route). The application is set up without any security-hardening middleware. Specifically:

- **No `helmet()`** — the proxy endpoint returns fetched HTML/text to the client without setting security headers (`X-Content-Type-Options`, `X-Frame-Options`, `Content-Security-Policy`, etc.). Since line 7 sends arbitrary third-party content directly via `res.send(body)`, the browser will interpret it according to its default content-type sniffing rules, potentially rendering malicious HTML.
- **No CORS configuration** — the proxy is open to cross-origin requests by default behavior.

This is especially dangerous in combination with SEC-001: an attacker can point the proxy at a URL returning malicious HTML, and the server will serve it under its own origin.

**Remediation:**

```typescript
import helmet from "helmet";
import cors from "cors";

const app = express();
app.use(helmet());
app.use(cors({ origin: "https://your-frontend.example.com" }));
```

Additionally, explicitly set `Content-Type` on the proxy response to prevent content-type sniffing attacks:

```typescript
res.set("Content-Type", "text/plain");
res.send(body);
```

---

## Summary

| ID | Severity | Title |
|---|---|---|
| SEC-001 | Critical | SSRF via unvalidated user-controlled URL in `fetch()` |
| SEC-002 | Medium | Missing security middleware (helmet, CORS) on Express app serving third-party content |

## Overall Score: **12 / 100**

## Verdict: **FAIL**

The code implements an open, unauthenticated proxy with no input validation, no URL allowlisting, and no security headers. SEC-001 alone is a critical vulnerability that enables full-read SSRF against the internal network and cloud metadata services. This code must not be deployed in any environment without the recommended remediations.

# Cybersecurity Evaluation

## Findings

### CYBER-001: Prototype Pollution via `deepMerge`

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Rule ID** | CYBER-001 |
| **Severity** | **Critical** |
| **CWE** | CWE-1321 (Prototype Pollution) |
| **OWASP** | A03:2021 – Injection |
| **Confidence** | 99% |

**Description:**

The `deepMerge` function on **line 2** iterates over `source` keys using `for...in` without checking `hasOwnProperty`, and critically, it performs **no filtering of dangerous property names** like `__proto__`, `constructor`, or `prototype`. On **line 13**, `req.body` (attacker-controlled JSON) is passed directly as the `source` argument.

An attacker can send a payload that pollutes `Object.prototype`, affecting **every object** in the Node.js process for the lifetime of that instance:

**Exploit:**

```http
POST /config HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "__proto__": {
    "isAdmin": true,
