The `jwt.sign()` call includes no `expiresIn` option. The resulting token is valid **forever** — if it is ever leaked (logs, browser history, stolen device, XSS in a downstream consumer), an attacker has permanent access with no revocation mechanism. This is not a theoretical concern; it is a concrete, exploitable misconfiguration in the code as written.

