### SEC-001: Open Redirect via Unvalidated `returnTo` Parameter

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| **Severity** | **High** |
| **CWE** | [CWE-601: URL Redirection to Untrusted Site ('Open Redirect')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html) |
| **Location** | Line 2–4 (`/login/callback` handler) |
| **Confidence** | 95% |

**Evidence:** The `returnTo` query parameter (line 2) is cast to a string and passed directly to `res.redirect()` on line 4 with no URL validation. An attacker can craft a URL like:

```
/login/callback?returnTo=https://evil.com/phishing
```

The server will issue a `302` redirect to the attacker-controlled domain. The fallback `"/dashboard"` only applies when the parameter is absent/empty — it does not constrain the parameter's value when present.

**Remediation:** Validate that the redirect target is a relative path or belongs to an explicit allowlist of trusted origins.

```typescript
import { URL } from "url";

function isSafeRedirect(target: string, trustedHost: string): boolean {
  if (target.startsWith("/") && !target.startsWith("//")) {
    return true; // relative path
  }
  try {
    const parsed = new URL(target);
    return parsed.host === trustedHost;
  } catch {
    return false;
  }
}

app.get("/login/callback", (req, res) => {
  const returnTo = req.query.returnTo as string || "/dashboard";
  const safeTarget = isSafeRedirect(returnTo, req.hostname) ? returnTo : "/dashboard";
  // Authenticate user...
  res.redirect(safeTarget);
});
```

---

### SEC-002: Open Redirect via Unvalidated `next` Parameter

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| **Severity** | **High** |
| **CWE** | [CWE-601: URL Redirection to Untrusted Site ('Open Redirect')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html) |
| **Location** | Line 8 (`/logout` handler) |
| **Confidence** | 95% |

**Evidence:** `req.query.next` on line 8 is passed directly to `res.redirect()` with zero validation. This is strictly worse than the `/login/callback` case because there is not even a default fallback — if the parameter is `undefined`, `res.redirect(undefined)` will throw at runtime. When present, any external URL is honoured:

```
/logout?next=https://evil.com/credential-harvest
```

This is especially dangerous on a logout endpoint because users have a lower guard ("I just logged out"), making phishing via a fake login page highly effective.

**Remediation:** Apply the same origin validation and provide a safe default.

```typescript
app.get("/logout", (req, res) => {
  req.session.destroy(() => {
    const next = req.query.next as string || "/";
    const safeTarget = isSafeRedirect(next, req.hostname) ? next : "/";
    res.redirect(safeTarget);
  });
});
```

