# Concurrency Review: `getOrderSummaries`

## Findings

### CONC-001: Sequential Await in Loop — N+1 Query Pattern Blocking the Event Loop Unnecessarily

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Rule ID** | CONC-007 |
| **Severity** | Medium |
| **Location** | Lines 6–13 (the `for (const order of orders)` loop) |

**Description:**

Every iteration of the outer loop (line 6) sequentially awaits two database queries (lines 7–8), and the inner loop (lines 9–11) sequentially awaits one query per item. These are independent I/O operations across different orders that have no data dependency on each other, yet they are serialized. For $N$ orders with an average of $M$ items each, this produces $N \times (2 + M)$ sequential round-trips to the database.

This is a **Promise/Future Handling** issue (criterion #7): independent operations are not parallelized with `Promise.all` or equivalent, and no semaphore/rate-limiter is used to justify the serialization.

**Concrete evidence:**
- Line 7: `await getRepository(Customer).findOne(...)` — depends only on `order.customerId`, not on results from prior iterations.
- Line 8: `await getRepository(OrderItem).find(...)` — depends only on `order.id`, not on prior iterations.
