- **Evidence**: Each message is processed sequentially — `await processOrder(payload)` followed by `await sqs.send(new DeleteMessageCommand(...))` — inside a serial `for...of` loop. With up to 10 messages per batch, the compute resource (Lambda, EC2, ECS) sits idle waiting on I/O for each message one at a time.
- **Impact**: If `processOrder` takes 200ms on average plus ~50ms for the delete call, processing 10 messages takes ~2.5s serially vs ~250ms with concurrent processing — a **10x throughput reduction** on the same compute resource. At scale, this means provisioning 10x more consumers or accepting 10x higher latency.
- **Recommendation**: Process messages concurrently within each batch using `Promise.allSettled`:
  ```typescript
  await Promise.allSettled(Messages.map(async (msg) => {
    const payload = JSON.parse(msg.Body!);
    await processOrder(payload);
    await sqs.send(new DeleteMessageCommand({
      QueueUrl: QUEUE_URL,
