| **Confidence** | 95% |

**Evidence:** Line 5 — `sub.webhookUrl` is fetched directly without any validation or allowlist check:

```typescript
await fetch(sub.webhookUrl, {
```

The `webhookUrl` value originates from the database (`db.getSubscribers`), but it was ultimately supplied by a subscriber at registration time — making it user-controlled. There is no validation visible in this code path before `fetch()` is invoked, meaning an attacker who registers a malicious webhook URL can force the server to issue HTTP requests to:

- **Internal network hosts** (`http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/` for cloud metadata, `http://localhost:6379/` for Redis, `http://10.0.0.x/admin`)
- **Localhost services** running on the same machine
- **Arbitrary external hosts** (using the server as a proxy/amplifier)

This is a textbook SSRF vulnerability. Even with a 30-second timeout, each request is fully issued to the attacker-controlled destination.

**Remediation:**

```typescript
import { URL } from "url";
import ipaddr from "ipaddr.js";
import dns from "dns/promises";

async function validateWebhookUrl(rawUrl: string): Promise<boolean> {
  const parsed = new URL(rawUrl);

  // Only allow HTTPS
  if (parsed.protocol !== "https:") return false;

  // Resolve hostname and reject private/reserved IPs
  const addresses = await dns.resolve4(parsed.hostname);
  for (const addr of addresses) {
    const ip = ipaddr.parse(addr);
    const range = ip.range();
    if (range !== "unicast") return false; // rejects private, loopback, link-local, etc.
  }
  return true;
}

// In the handler:
for (const sub of subscribers) {
  if (!(await validateWebhookUrl(sub.webhookUrl))) {
    continue; // skip dangerous URLs
  }
  await fetch(sub.webhookUrl, { /* ... */ });
}
```

