### PERF-003: N+1 Recursive Queries Without Depth Limit
**Severity:** High
**Location:** Lines 23–32

```javascript
async function getChildren(parentId) {
  const { rows } = await pool.query(
    'SELECT * FROM categories WHERE parent_id = $1', [parentId]
  );
  for (const row of rows) {
    row.children = await getChildren(row.id);
  }
  return rows;
}
```

**Evidence:** This issues one `SELECT` query **per node** in the category tree. A tree with 500 categories produces 500+ sequential round-trips to the database. Each round-trip has network latency (~1–5 ms local, more remote), so 500 nodes × 2 ms = ~1 second of pure I/O wait. There is also no depth limit, so a malformed data cycle (`A → B → A`) causes infinite recursion, stack overflow, and connection pool exhaustion.

**Impact:** O(N) sequential database queries where N is number of nodes; potential stack overflow on cyclic data; connection pool starvation under concurrent requests.

**Recommendation:**

```javascript
app.get('/api/categories/:id/tree', async (req, res) => {
  const { rows } = await pool.query(`
    WITH RECURSIVE cat_tree AS (
      SELECT *, 1 AS depth FROM categories WHERE id = $1
      UNION ALL
      SELECT c.*, ct.depth + 1
      FROM categories c
      JOIN cat_tree ct ON c.parent_id = ct.id
      WHERE ct.depth < 20  -- depth limit prevents cycles
    )
    SELECT id, name, parent_id, depth FROM cat_tree
    ORDER BY depth, name
  `, [req.params.id]);

  // Build tree in-memory from flat result (single query)
  const map = new Map();
  const root = [];
  for (const row of rows) {
    row.children = [];
    map.set(row.id, row);
    if (row.depth === 1) root.push(row);
    else map.get(row.parent_id)?.children.push(row);
  }
  res.json(root);
});
```

This replaces N sequential queries with a single recursive CTE, includes a depth guard, and projects only needed columns.

