### CACHE-002: Missing Cache Invalidation on User Update
**Severity:** Critical
**Location:** Lines 15–20 (`PUT /api/users/:id`)

**Evidence:** The `PUT /api/users/:id` handler calls `db.updateUser()` but never calls `cache.delete(`user:${req.params.id}`)` or `setInCache(...)` with the updated value. Meanwhile, `GET /api/users/:id` (line 22) serves whatever is in the cache. After an update, the cache entry `user:<id>` contains the pre-update data and will be served indefinitely — there is no TTL or any other mechanism to expire it.

Additionally, the `all-users` cache key (line 32) also becomes stale after any user update and is never invalidated.

**Impact:** Every read after a write returns stale data until the process restarts. This is a textbook cache invalidation bug with direct data-correctness consequences.

**Recommendation:**
```javascript
app.put('/api/users/:id', async (req, res) => {
  const user = await db.updateUser(req.params.id, req.body);
  cache.delete(`user:${req.params.id}`);
  cache.delete('all-users');
  res.json(user);
});
```

