# Scalability Evaluation

## Findings

### SCALE-001: Unbounded In-Memory Collection Growth
**Severity**: High
**Location**: Line 11 (`setProducts(prev => [...prev, ...data])`)

**Evidence**: The `setProducts(prev => [...prev, ...data])` pattern appends every page of results into a single, ever-growing array. As the user scrolls, this array grows without bound — at 1000 pages of 20 items each, you have 20,000 product objects in React state, each re-rendered on every state update.

**Impact**: At scale (large product catalogs), this causes:
- Linear memory growth proportional to scroll depth — eventually exhausting browser memory on mobile devices.
- Increasingly expensive React reconciliation — each new page triggers a diff of the entire `products` array.
- Array spread (`[...prev, ...data]`) allocates a new array copying all previous elements on every page load — $O(n)$ per page fetch, $O(n^2)$ total memory allocations over the session.

**Recommendation**: Implement windowed/virtualized rendering (e.g., `react-window` or `@tanstack/virtual`) so only visible items are in the DOM. Optionally evict off-screen pages from state to cap memory usage.

