### TEST-001: Silent Failure by Design — Untypeable Event Names Prevent Any Test from Catching Mismatches
**Severity**: High
**Category**: Testability
**Location**: Lines 1–17 (EventBus class), Lines 20–26 (consumer registrations)

**Evidence**: The `EventBus` class uses `string` for event names (line 5) and `Function`/`any` for handler signatures (lines 5, 11). This directly causes the demonstrated bug on line 26: `bus.emit("user-Created", ...)` matches **zero** of the four registered handlers (`"user-created"`, `"User-Created"`, `"userCreated"`, `"user_created"`). The `emit` method on line 13 silently returns an empty array via `|| []`, producing no error, no warning, and no observable side effect.

**Why this is a real testability defect**: There is no mechanism — at compile time or runtime — to detect that an emitted event has no subscribers. A unit test asserting that `emit("user-Created", ...)` triggers `sendWelcomeEmail` would catch this *specific* instance, but the architecture guarantees the same class of bug will recur for every new event name, because:
1. No type constraint limits the set of valid event names.
2. No runtime feedback (return value, error, logged warning) signals a zero-handler emit.

**Recommendation**: Make the EventBus generic over a typed event map so the compiler prevents mismatched names and payload shapes:

```typescript
type EventMap = {
  "user-created": { userId: string };
  "order-updated": { orderId: string; status: string };
};

export class TypedEventBus<T extends Record<string, unknown>> {
  private handlers = new Map<keyof T, ((data: any) => void)[]>();

  on<K extends keyof T>(event: K, handler: (data: T[K]) => void): void {
    const list = this.handlers.get(event) || [];
    list.push(handler);
    this.handlers.set(event, list);
  }

  emit<K extends keyof T>(event: K, data: T[K]): void {
    const list = this.handlers.get(event) || [];
    list.forEach(h => h(data));
  }
}

// Now a typo is a compile-time error:
const bus = new TypedEventBus<EventMap>();
bus.on("user-created", (data) => sendWelcomeEmail(data)); // ✅
// bus.on("User-Created", (data) => {}); // ❌ compile error
```

A corresponding test becomes meaningful and stable:

```typescript
it("invokes all registered handlers on emit", () => {
  const bus = new TypedEventBus<EventMap>();
  const spy = vi.fn();
  bus.on("user-created", spy);
  bus.emit("user-created", { userId: "123" });
  expect(spy).toHaveBeenCalledWith({ userId: "123" });
});
```

