```java
import java.io.*;
import java.net.*;

public class DataReceiver {
    public Object receiveData(int port) throws Exception {
        ServerSocket server = new ServerSocket(port);
        Socket socket = server.accept();
        ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(socket.getInputStream());

        // Allowlist only the specific types you expect
        ois.setObjectInputFilter(filterInfo -> {
            Class<?> clazz = filterInfo.serialClass();
            if (clazz == null) {
                return ObjectInputFilter.Status.UNDECIDED;
            }
            if (clazz == MyExpectedClass.class) {
                return ObjectInputFilter.Status.ALLOWED;
            }
            return ObjectInputFilter.Status.REJECTED;
        });

        Object obj = ois.readObject();
        ois.close();
        socket.close();
        server.close();
        return obj;
    }
}
```

**Option B — Replace Java serialization entirely:**

Use a data format that does not instantiate arbitrary types, such as JSON (Jackson/Gson) or Protocol Buffers:

```java
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;
// ...
BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(
    new InputStreamReader(socket.getInputStream()));
MyExpectedClass obj = new ObjectMapper()
    .readValue(reader, MyExpectedClass.class);
```

Option B is the strongly preferred approach. Native Java serialization over a network socket is inherently dangerous and difficult to secure fully even with filters.

