**Description:**

The `username` and `password` parameters are concatenated directly into the LDAP search filter on line 6:

```java
String filter = "(&(uid=" + username + ")(userPassword=" + password + "))";
```

There is zero input validation or escaping. An attacker can inject arbitrary LDAP filter syntax to bypass authentication entirely.

**Exploit Scenario:**

An attacker supplies:
- `username`: `*)(uid=*))(|(uid=*`
- `password`: anything

This produces the filter:
```
(&(uid=*)(uid=*))(|(uid=*)(userPassword=anything))
```

The first complete filter `(&(uid=*)(uid=*))` matches **every user** in the directory. Since `results.hasMore()` on line 10 only checks if at least one result exists, the attacker is authenticated without knowing any valid credentials.

Even simpler — `username = *` would match all entries with a `uid` attribute:
```
(&(uid=*)(userPassword=anything))
```

Combined with password `*`:
```
(&(uid=*)(userPassword=*))
```
This matches every user that has both fields, granting full authentication bypass.

**Remediation:**

Escape all LDAP special characters (`*`, `(`, `)`, `\`, `NUL`, `/`) in user-supplied values before embedding them in the filter. Use a dedicated encoding method:

```java
import javax.naming.ldap.Rfc2253LdapName; // or a utility like Spring's LdapEncoder

public boolean authenticate(String username, String password) {
    try {
        // Escape LDAP filter special characters
        String safeUsername = escapeLdapFilter(username);
        String safePassword = escapeLdapFilter(password);

        String filter = "(&(uid=" + safeUsername + ")(userPassword=" + safePassword + "))";
        SearchControls sc = new SearchControls();
        sc.setSearchScope(SearchControls.SUBTREE_SCOPE);
        NamingEnumeration<?> results = ctx.search("dc=example,dc=com", filter, sc);
        return results.hasMore();
    } catch (Exception e) {
        return false;
    }
}

// RFC 4515 compliant escaping
private static String escapeLdapFilter(String input) {
    if (input == null) return "";
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
    for (char c : input.toCharArray()) {
        switch (c) {
            case '\\': sb.append("\\5c"); break;
            case '*':  sb.append("\\2a"); break;
            case '(':  sb.append("\\28"); break;
            case ')':  sb.append("\\29"); break;
            case '\0': sb.append("\\00"); break;
            default:   sb.append(c);
        }
    }
    return sb.toString();
}
```

**Better yet**, do not embed the password in the search filter at all. The secure pattern is to search by username, then perform an LDAP **bind** with the found DN and the supplied password:

```java
public boolean authenticate(String username, String password) {
    try {
        String safeUsername = escapeLdapFilter(username);
        String filter = "(uid=" + safeUsername + ")";
        SearchControls sc = new SearchControls();
        sc.setSearchScope(SearchControls.SUBTREE_SCOPE);
        NamingEnumeration<SearchResult> results = ctx.search("dc=example,dc=com", filter, sc);
        if (!results.hasMore()) return false;
        String userDn = results.next().getNameInNamespace();

        // Authenticate via LDAP bind
        Hashtable<String, String> env = new Hashtable<>();
        env.put(Context.INITIAL_CONTEXT_FACTORY, "com.sun.jndi.ldap.LdapCtxFactory");
