The CORS configuration sets `origin: "*"` (allow all origins) combined with `credentials: true`. This instructs the server to accept cross-origin requests with cookies/credentials from **any** origin. While most browsers will actually block this specific combination (the CORS spec forbids `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` with `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`), this configuration signals a fundamental misunderstanding of cross-origin security policy and represents a dangerous intent. If the `cors` middleware or a future version resolves this by reflecting the request's `Origin` header instead (which some configurations do), any malicious site could issue authenticated cross-origin requests to `/api/transfer` — enabling full CSRF-style attacks on the funds transfer endpoint.

**Remediation:**

Restrict the allowed origin to known, trusted domains, and only enable credentials when necessary:

```typescript
app.use(cors({
  origin: "https://your-trusted-frontend.example.com",
