### CYBER-001: Unsafe Deserialization of Untrusted Network Data (CWE-502)

**Severity:** 🔴 **CRITICAL**

**Location:** Lines 17–18

```java
ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(socket.getInputStream());
Object obj = ois.readObject();
```

**Description:**
The code accepts arbitrary TCP connections on port 9999 and directly deserializes the incoming byte stream using `java.io.ObjectInputStream.readObject()` with **zero filtering or validation**. This is a textbook insecure deserialization vulnerability — one of the most dangerous classes of Java vulnerabilities (OWASP A08:2021 – Software and Data Integrity Failures).

**Exploit Path:**
An attacker connects to port 9999 and sends a crafted serialized object payload (e.g., generated by [ysoserial](https://github.com/frohoff/ysoserial)) that exploits gadget chains present on the classpath (e.g., Commons Collections, Spring, etc.). Upon `readObject()`, the JVM instantiates attacker-controlled objects and invokes their deserialization callbacks (`readObject`, `readResolve`, `finalize`, etc.), achieving **Remote Code Execution (RCE)** before `processObject()` is ever called.

This is not theoretical — insecure Java deserialization has been exploited in the wild against Apache Commons Collections, WebLogic, Jenkins, JBoss, and many others (CVE-2015-4852, CVE-2017-10271, CVE-2019-17571, among dozens).

**Additionally**, line 30–31 compounds the risk:
```java
if (obj instanceof Command) {
    ((Command) obj).execute();
}
```
Even if deserialization itself were safe, blindly calling `.execute()` on any deserialized object implementing `Command` allows an attacker to provide an arbitrary `Command` implementation with malicious logic in `execute()`.

**Remediation:**

1. **Preferred: Do not use Java native serialization for untrusted input.** Replace with a safe data format (JSON, Protocol Buffers, etc.):
    ```java
    // Example using Jackson for JSON
    ObjectMapper mapper = new ObjectMapper();
    MyExpectedType obj = mapper.readValue(socket.getInputStream(), MyExpectedType.class);
    ```

2. **If `ObjectInputStream` is required**, use a deserialization filter (JEP 290, available since Java 9, backported to 8u121+):
    ```java
    ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(socket.getInputStream());
    ois.setObjectInputFilter(filterInfo -> {
        if (filterInfo.serialClass() != null
            && !filterInfo.serialClass().getName().equals("com.example.AllowedCommand")) {
            return ObjectInputFilter.Status.REJECTED;
        }
        return ObjectInputFilter.Status.ALLOWED;
    });
    Object obj = ois.readObject();
    ```

3. Consider using [Apache Commons IO's `ValidatingObjectInputStream`](https://commons.apache.org/proper/commons-io/javadocs/api-2.11.0/org/apache/commons/io/serialization/ValidatingObjectInputStream.html) to whitelist allowed classes.

**References:**
- CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data
- OWASP Top 10 2021 — A08: Software and Data Integrity Failures
- CVE-2015-4852 (Apache Commons Collections gadget chains)

