**Description:**
The `Command` interface (line 34) is `Serializable` with a single `void execute()` method. Any attacker-supplied class on the classpath (or delivered through the deserialized stream itself in certain classloader configurations) that implements this interface will have its `execute()` method invoked unconditionally.

This is a **class-of-service confusion / use of externally-controlled input to select classes** vulnerability. Even if CYBER-001 were partially mitigated by allowing only `Command` implementations, the attacker controls which implementation is instantiated and what its `execute()` method does.

**Exploit Path:**
An attacker crafts a serialized object that implements `Command` and contains a malicious `execute()` body (e.g., `Runtime.getRuntime().exec("...")`). The server deserializes it and calls `execute()`, achieving arbitrary command execution.

**Remediation:**
- Replace the open `Command` interface pattern with an explicit command registry that maps safe, enumerated identifiers to known-good handler implementations. Never invoke methods on attacker-supplied object instances.

```java
// Safe pattern: command dispatch by name
Map<String, Runnable> handlers = Map.of("ping", () -> System.out.println("pong"));
String commandName = readCommandName(socket); // read a simple string, not an object
Runnable handler = handlers.get(commandName);
if (handler != null) handler.run();
```

**References:**
- CWE-470: Use of Externally-Controlled Input to Select Classes or Code
- CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data

