# Security Architecture Review — `DataReceiver.java`

## Findings

### SEC-001: Unsafe Deserialization of Untrusted Network Data

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Severity** | **Critical** |
| **CWE** | [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html) |
| **Confidence** | 99% |
| **Location** | Lines 17–18 |

**Description:**

The code opens a `ServerSocket` on port 9999 (line 9) and directly deserializes arbitrary objects from the raw network stream using `java.io.ObjectInputStream` (lines 17–18):

```java
ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(socket.getInputStream());
Object obj = ois.readObject();
```

There is **no deserialization filter**, no allowlist of permitted classes, and no validation whatsoever before `readObject()` is invoked. Any remote client that can reach port 9999 can send a crafted serialized byte stream containing gadget chains (e.g., from Commons Collections, Spring, or other libraries on the classpath) to achieve **Remote Code Execution (RCE)**.

This is the textbook example of the vulnerability class that led to widespread exploitation (Apache Commons Collections gadget chains, ysoserial payloads, etc.). The `Command.execute()` call on line 30 further amplifies the impact — if an attacker supplies a malicious `Command` implementation, it is explicitly executed.

**Evidence:**
- Line 9: `new ServerSocket(9999)` — unauthenticated network listener.
- Line 17: `new ObjectInputStream(socket.getInputStream())` — no filtering.
- Line 18: `ois.readObject()` — deserializes arbitrary classes.
- Line 30: `((Command) obj).execute()` — executes deserialized object behavior.

**Remediation:**

**Option A (Preferred): Eliminate Java serialization entirely.** Replace `ObjectInputStream` with a safe data format like JSON (Jackson, Gson) or Protocol Buffers.

```java
// Example: replace with Jackson JSON deserialization
ObjectMapper mapper = new ObjectMapper();
// Only allow known, safe types
mapper.activateDefaultTyping(
    mapper.getPolymorphicTypeValidator(),
    ObjectMapper.DefaultTyping.NON_FINAL
);
MyMessage msg = mapper.readValue(socket.getInputStream(), MyMessage.class);
```

**Option B (If Java serialization is required): Use an `ObjectInputFilter` (JEP 290, Java 9+).**

```java
ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(socket.getInputStream());
ois.setObjectInputFilter(info -> {
    if (info.serialClass() != null) {
        String name = info.serialClass().getName();
        // Strict allowlist of permitted classes
        if (name.equals("com.example.MyAllowedCommand")) {
            return ObjectInputFilter.Status.ALLOWED;
        }
        return ObjectInputFilter.Status.REJECTED;
    }
    // Limit depth and array sizes
    if (info.depth() > 5 || info.arrayLength() > 1000) {
        return ObjectInputFilter.Status.REJECTED;
    }
    return ObjectInputFilter.Status.UNDECIDED;
});
Object obj = ois.readObject();
```

**Option C (Java 8): Use Apache Commons IO `ValidatingObjectInputStream`.**

```java
ValidatingObjectInputStream vois = new ValidatingObjectInputStream(socket.getInputStream());
vois.accept(MyAllowedCommand.class);
Object obj = vois.readObject();
```

