### SEC-003: Insecure Data Flow — Unsanitized User Input in LDAP Filter

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| **Severity** | Critical |
| **CWE** | CWE-90 (LDAP Injection) |
| **Confidence** | 98% |
| **Location** | Line 21 |

**Note on scope:** While injection attacks are typically deferred to the CYBER judge, this finding is reported under the **insecure data flow** criterion (evaluation criterion #1: "user-controlled inputs used directly in... operations without validation"). The `username` and `password` parameters flow directly from user input into the LDAP search filter with zero sanitization.

**Evidence:**

```java
String filter = "(&(uid=" + username + ")(userPassword=" + password + "))";
```

Both `username` and `password` are concatenated directly. An attacker can supply:

- `username = "admin)(|(uid=*"` → produces `(&(uid=admin)(|(uid=*)(userPassword=anything))` — authentication bypass.

**Recommendation:**

Even after switching to bind-based authentication (SEC-002), any remaining search filters must sanitize inputs by escaping LDAP special characters (`*`, `(`, `)`, `\`, `NUL`):

```java
private static String sanitizeLdapFilter(String input) {
    if (input == null) return "";
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
    for (char c : input.toCharArray()) {
        switch (c) {
            case '\\': sb.append("\\5c"); break;
            case '*':  sb.append("\\2a"); break;
            case '(':  sb.append("\\28"); break;
            case ')':  sb.append("\\29"); break;
            case '\0': sb.append("\\00"); break;
            default:   sb.append(c);
        }
    }
    return sb.toString();
}
```

