Because the server reflects `evil.example.com` back in `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` **and** sets `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`, the browser permits the cross-origin credentialed request. The attacker silently exfiltrates the victim's profile and SSN.

This is not a theoretical concern — it is a textbook CORS misconfiguration that completely bypasses the Same-Origin Policy for any authenticated endpoint.

**Remediation:**

Replace the reflected-origin middleware with an explicit allowlist:

```javascript
const cors = require('cors');

const allowedOrigins = ['https://app.example.com'];

app.use(cors({
  origin: (origin, callback) => {
    if (!origin || allowedOrigins.includes(origin)) {
      callback(null, true);
    } else {
      callback(new Error('Not allowed by CORS'));
    }
  },
  credentials: true
}));
```

