### CYBER-001: LDAP Injection via Unsanitized User Input

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Rule ID** | CYBER-001 |
| **Severity** | **Critical** |
| **CWE** | CWE-90 (LDAP Injection) |
| **OWASP** | A03:2021 – Injection |
| **Line** | 11 |
| **Confidence** | 99% |

**Description:**

On **line 11**, the `username` parameter from the query string is directly concatenated into the LDAP search filter without any sanitization or escaping:

```csharp
searcher.Filter = "(uid=" + username + ")";
```

The `username` value comes from `[FromQuery]` (line 9), which is fully attacker-controlled. An attacker can inject arbitrary LDAP filter syntax to alter the query's logic.

**Exploit Scenario:**

An attacker sends:
```
GET /user?username=*)(%26(objectClass=*))
```

This transforms the filter into:
```
(uid=*)(|(objectClass=*))
```

This bypasses the intended single-user lookup and can:

1. **Enumerate all users** — by injecting `*` as the username, `(uid=*)` matches every entry in the directory.
2. **Extract sensitive attributes** — by crafting filters that conditionally match, an attacker can perform blind LDAP injection to brute-force attribute values (e.g., passwords, group memberships).
3. **Bypass authentication logic** — if this pattern is used elsewhere for auth decisions, injecting `*)(|(uid=*` would always return a result, granting unauthorized access.

**Remediation:**

Sanitize the input by escaping LDAP special characters (`*`, `(`, `)`, `\`, `NUL`, `/`) per RFC 4515 before embedding it in the filter string.

```csharp
[HttpGet("user")]
public IActionResult FindUser([FromQuery] string username)
{
    if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(username))
        return BadRequest("Username is required.");

    // Escape LDAP special characters per RFC 4515
    string sanitized = EscapeLdapFilterValue(username);

    var searcher = new DirectorySearcher();
    searcher.Filter = $"(uid={sanitized})";
    var result = searcher.FindOne();

    if (result == null)
        return NotFound();

    return Ok(result.Properties["cn"][0]);
}

private static string EscapeLdapFilterValue(string value)
{
    var sb = new System.Text.StringBuilder();
    foreach (char c in value)
    {
        switch (c)
        {
            case '\\': sb.Append("\\5c"); break;
            case '*':  sb.Append("\\2a"); break;
            case '(':  sb.Append("\\28"); break;
            case ')':  sb.Append("\\29"); break;
            case '\0': sb.Append("\\00"); break;
            default:   sb.Append(c); break;
        }
    }
    return sb.ToString();
}
```

Additionally, consider applying an allowlist regex (e.g., `^[a-zA-Z0-9._-]+$`) to reject obviously malicious input before it reaches the LDAP layer.

