**Description:**

`_.merge()` recursively merges properties from source objects into a target object, including nested keys like `__proto__`, `constructor`, and `prototype`. On line 11, `req.body` — which is fully attacker-controlled — is passed directly as a source argument to `_.merge()`. This allows an attacker to inject properties into `Object.prototype`, polluting the prototype chain for all objects in the Node.js process.

**Exploit:**

```http
POST /settings HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json

{"__proto__": {"isAdmin": true}}
```

After this request, **every** plain object created in the process will inherit `isAdmin === true`:

```javascript
const obj = {};
console.log(obj.isAdmin); // true
```

This can lead to:
- **Authorization bypass** if downstream code checks properties like `obj.isAdmin` or `obj.role`.
- **Remote Code Execution** in certain contexts (e.g., if a child process spawn or template engine later reads polluted properties like `shell`, `env`, or template directives from `Object.prototype`).
- **Denial of Service** by polluting properties that break framework internals.

This is not theoretical — prototype pollution via `lodash.merge` is a well-documented, repeatedly exploited vulnerability class (CVE-2018-16487, CVE-2018-3721).

**Remediation:**

**Option A — Sanitize input before merging (recommended minimal fix):**

```typescript
function sanitize(obj: unknown): Record<string, unknown> {
  const raw = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(obj)); // strips __proto__ via JSON round-trip
  delete raw["__proto__"];
  delete raw["constructor"];
  delete raw["prototype"];
  return raw;
}

app.post("/settings", (req, res) => {
  const settings = _.merge({}, defaults, sanitize(req.body));
  res.json(settings);
});
```

**Option B — Use a safe shallow merge (preferred if deep merge is not needed):**

```typescript
app.post("/settings", (req, res) => {
  const allowed = ["theme", "lang"];
  const userSettings = Object.fromEntries(
    Object.entries(req.body).filter(([key]) => allowed.includes(key))
  );
  const settings = { ...defaults, ...userSettings };
  res.json(settings);
});
```

Option B is strongly preferred because it applies an allowlist, eliminating the entire attack surface. Only explicitly permitted fields are accepted from the user — this is defense-in-depth against both prototype pollution and mass assignment.

**Option C — Replace `lodash.merge` with a prototype-pollution-safe deep merge utility:**

Libraries like [`deepmerge`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/deepmerge) or [`defu`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/defu) are designed to be safe against prototype pollution by default.

